From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 05:48:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: one browser window per CRTC, kept through every
 hotplug

A desk of three monitors showed the chrome on one of them. `--app=` opens a
single window; `ScreenManager::FindWindowAt` binds a window to a display
controller only on an EXACT rectangle match against
`gfx::Rect(controller->origin(), controller->GetModeSize())`; and one window
cannot be two rectangles. So the other two CRTCs had no window, a modeset lit
them, and nothing was ever scanned out to them.

The answer is one browser window per CRTC, and this is the call that starts
keeping it that way. `StartShellWindows` sits beside `StartCommandSocket` in
PostBrowserStart for the same two reasons: the shell's own window exists by
that point -- `browser_creator_->Start` has run -- and the windows this opens
are copies of it, its profile on its URL, so there is nothing to copy before
it is there.

A RECONCILIATION RATHER THAN A SETUP STEP, because monitors come and go. The
decision is `ShellWindowsFor` in //components/domicile:shell_windows, which
is where its tests are: what displays have no window, and what windows have no
display. It is run at startup and again from a display::DisplayObserver on
every add, every removal and every bounds change.

Two rules in it are the difference between a desktop and a dead session, and
both are tested there rather than argued here. THE LAST WINDOW IS NEVER
CLOSED: closing it is the browser exiting, and the browser exiting is the
session ending, so a lid shut on a laptop with nothing plugged in would log
the user out rather than blank a screen. AND WINDOWS OPEN BEFORE THEY CLOSE:
a dock swapped under a running desktop closes as many windows as it opens, and
closing first would pass through zero.

A display that merely MOVED or changed mode is neither an open nor a close --
the window is the right window with the wrong rectangle, which the exact-rect
rule above makes a black screen with a clean log. That one is a SetBounds onto
the display's own bounds.

`display_id` on BrowserWindowCreateParams is `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)`, so
there is no asking for a window on a named display on this build. A window
lands on the display its rectangle is on, which is what creating it at the
display's own bounds arranges, and `kFullscreen` from the start rather than a
toggle afterwards keeps it from drawing a frame on the wrong monitor first.
---
 chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
index f520f2a..29aa3b8 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.cc
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 #include "chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main.h"
 
 #include "chrome/browser/domicile/domicile_command_socket.h"
+#include "chrome/browser/domicile/domicile_shell_windows.h"
 
 #include <memory>
 #include <string>
@@ -1718,6 +1719,13 @@ void ChromeBrowserMainParts::PostBrowserStart() {
   // would be missing in the one case it exists for.
   domicile::StartCommandSocket();
 
+  // Domicile's engine fork: one shell window per display, kept that way as
+  // monitors come and go. Here for the same two reasons as the socket above --
+  // the shell's own window exists by now, and these are copies of it -- and
+  // after it rather than before because a desk that comes up wrong is a desk
+  // somebody will want to point `load_shell` at.
+  domicile::StartShellWindows();
+
   for (auto& chrome_extra_part : chrome_extra_parts_) {
     chrome_extra_part->PostBrowserStart();
   }
